<p class="lead">Gleannloch Farms is a master-planned community, and it shows in the consistency of its streetscapes, landscaping and architecture. That consistency is a good starting point for landscape lighting, but it also means plans should respect community standards and fit the character of the street. Spring Landscape Lighting designs systems for Gleannloch Farms homes that look refined and intentional, work within HOA expectations, and suit both newer builds and the larger estate-style lots found in some sections.</p>

<h2>Working with HOA guidelines</h2>
<p>Master-planned communities typically have deed restrictions and an architectural review process for exterior changes. Requirements vary, so the first step is to check your community guidelines and find out whether lighting needs a submittal. In our experience, associations are generally comfortable with low-voltage landscape lighting that is warm, shielded and modest in output. What tends to raise concerns is glare, very cool or colored light, and fixtures aimed toward neighbors or the street.</p>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Check the guidelines.</strong> Review your deed restrictions or ask the HOA whether landscape lighting requires approval.</li>
  <li><strong>Get a plan on paper.</strong> We provide a layout with fixture types, locations and color temperature that can accompany a submittal.</li>
  <li><strong>Install and adjust.</strong> Once approved, we install and fine-tune after dark to keep light on your property.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Newer builds and young landscapes</h2>
<p>Many homes here are relatively new, with brick and stone elevations and landscapes that are still filling in. That calls for planning ahead. Young trees will grow, beds will thicken, and what looks balanced in year one can be swallowed by year four. We leave slack in the wire, choose adjustable fixtures and size the transformer with room for additions. Strong elevations make a good case for <a href="/services/architectural-lighting/">architectural lighting</a> as the first layer, with trees added as they mature.</p>

<h2>Long driveways and estate lots</h2>
<p>Some sections of Gleannloch Farms include larger lots with long driveways and deep setbacks. These properties need a different approach than a typical subdivision lot:</p>
<div class="grid-cards">
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Mark the key points</h3><p>Light the entry, curves, grade changes and arrival court instead of lining the whole drive.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Plan for voltage drop</h3><p>Long wire runs need the right gauge, multi-tap transformers or a second transformer near the far end.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Use zones</h3><p>Let the drive and entry run on a different schedule than the facade and backyard.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Our <a href="/services/pathway-driveway-lighting/">pathway and driveway lighting</a> page covers fixture choices in more detail, and you can check wire runs with the <a href="/tools/transformer-sizing-calculator/">transformer and voltage drop calculator</a>.</p>

<p>Whether your home is new or has been here a while, Spring Landscape Lighting can design a plan that looks like it belongs on the street.</p>

